WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.785
A full house looks like success.

00:00:02.785 --> 00:00:07.485
But does a bigger crowd always mean a better business?

00:00:07.485 --> 00:00:13.839
Let us explore a miniature concert, using invented numbers in US dollars.

00:00:13.989 --> 00:00:21.129
At a ticket price of twenty dollars, one hundred and fifty guests attend.

00:00:21.129 --> 00:00:23.672
Revenue is three thousand.

00:00:23.672 --> 00:00:31.497
After production, venue and per-person costs, profit is seven hundred and fifty.

00:00:31.647 --> 00:00:35.300
Raise the ticket price to thirty dollars.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.974
Now one hundred guests attend.

00:00:37.974 --> 00:00:42.251
Revenue stays at three thousand, but costs fall.

00:00:42.251 --> 00:00:44.835
Profit rises to one thousand.

00:00:44.985 --> 00:00:50.773
Now rent a thousand-place venue without changing interest.

00:00:50.773 --> 00:00:55.662
The crowd stays the same, while venue costs rise.

00:00:55.662 --> 00:00:59.753
The event loses twelve hundred and fifty.

00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:03.813
More capacity is not the same as more demand.

00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:05.985
Try your own assumptions.

00:01:05.985 --> 00:01:10.677
Follow the money, and remember: revenue is not profit.
